Task 1: Verify and Interpret SELinux Contexts for VM Workloads
Step 1.1: Check SELinux Status on a Worker Node
Open a debug session on a worker node:
oc debug node/worker-0
Inside the debug pod, change root into the host filesystem and check SELinux status:
chroot /host
sestatus
Expected output:

SELinux status:                 enabled
SELinuxfs mount:                /sys/fs/selinux
SELinux root directory:         /etc/selinux
Loaded policy name:              targeted
Current mode:                   enforcing
Mode from config file:          enforcing
Policy MLS status:               enabled
Policy deny_unknown status:      allowed
Memory protection checking:      actual (secure)
Max kernel policy version:       33
Current mode: enforcing confirms SELinux is actively blocking denied operations rather than only logging them (permissive). Exit the chroot and debug pod when done:

exit
exit
Step 1.2: Deploy a Test VM
Create a simple VirtualMachine so you have a live QEMU process to inspect:

cat > secure-vm.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: kubevirt.io/v1
kind: VirtualMachine
metadata:
  name: secure-vm
  namespace: default
  labels:
    app: secure-workload
spec:
  running: false
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        kubevirt.io/vm: secure-vm
    spec:
      domain:
        cpu:
          cores: 1
        devices:
          disks:
          - disk:
              bus: virtio
            name: containerdisk
          - disk:
              bus: virtio
            name: cloudinitdisk
          interfaces:
          - masquerade: {}
            name: default
        resources:
          requests:
            memory: 1Gi
      networks:
      - name: default
        pod: {}
      volumes:
      - containerDisk:
          image: quay.io/kubevirt/fedora-cloud-container-disk-demo
        name: containerdisk
      - cloudInitNoCloud:
          userData: |
            #cloud-config
            user: fedora
            password: fedora
            chpasswd: { expire: False }
        name: cloudinitdisk
EOF

oc apply -f secure-vm.yaml
Start the VM and wait until it reports as running:

oc patch vm secure-vm --type merge -p '{"spec":{"running":true}}'
oc get vmi secure-vm -w
Expected output progression: PHASE moves from Scheduling to Scheduled to Running. Press Ctrl+C once you see Running.

Step 1.3: Inspect the SELinux Context of the QEMU Process
Find the virt-launcher pod backing the VM, then inspect the process context from inside the pod:

VM_POD=$(oc get pods -l kubevirt.io/vm=secure-vm -o jsonpath='{.items[0].metadata.name}')
oc get pod $VM_POD -o jsonpath='{.metadata.name}{"\n"}'

oc exec $VM_POD -c compute -- ps -eZ | grep qemu
Expected output (fields may wrap, format is label uid pid tty time cmd):

system_u:system_r:container_t:s0:c123,c456   107   1   ?   00:02:14   qemu-kvm
How to read this SELinux context (user:role:type:level):

system_u - the SELinux user associated with the process, not the Linux user
system_r - the SELinux role, system_r indicates a system service role rather than an interactive login role
container_t - the SELinux type. This is the domain that determines what the process is allowed to do. Container-launched QEMU processes typically run under container_t (or a more specific virt_launcher_t-style type on some builds), which is confined by policy rules to only the resources needed to run a VM inside a pod
s0:c123,c456 - the MCS (Multi-Category Security) sensitivity and category set. Each pod is assigned a unique category pair (here c123,c456). This is the mechanism that prevents one pod's process from reading or writing another pod's files even though both may run under the same type: the categories must match for access to be allowed
Deliverable for Task 1: capture the sestatus output showing Current mode: enforcing, and the ps -eZ output line showing the qemu-kvm process with its full user:role:type:level context. Confirm that two different VM pods, if compared, show different category sets (cNNN) even though the type is the same.

Task 2: Apply an AppArmor Profile to a KubeVirt VM
Step 2.1: Verify AppArmor Availability
oc debug node/worker-0
chroot /host
systemctl is-active apparmor
aa-status
Expected output for aa-status starts with a summary line such as:

apparmor module is loaded.
X profiles are loaded.
X profiles are in enforce mode.
   ...
0 profiles are in complain mode.
0 processes have profiles defined.
Exit back out when finished:

exit
exit
Step 2.2: Create and Load a Custom AppArmor Profile
Write the profile file completely in one step on the worker node:

oc debug node/worker-0 -- chroot /host bash -c 'cat > /etc/apparmor.d/kubevirt-vm << "EOF"
#include <tunables/global>

profile kubevirt-vm flags=(attach_disconnected,mediate_deleted) {
  #include <abstractions/base>
  #include <abstractions/nameservice>

  # Allow basic system binaries to be read and executed
  /bin/** mr,
  /sbin/** mr,
  /usr/bin/** mr,
  /usr/sbin/** mr,
  /lib{,32,64}/** mr,
  /usr/lib{,32,64}/** mr,

  # Allow access to VM-specific paths
  /var/lib/kubelet/pods/** rw,
  /dev/kvm rw,
  /dev/vhost-net rw,
  /dev/net/tun rw,

  # Network access required for VM networking
  network inet stream,
  network inet dgram,
  network inet6 stream,
  network inet6 dgram,

  # Explicitly deny dangerous capabilities
  deny capability sys_admin,
  deny capability sys_module,
  deny capability sys_rawio,

  # Capabilities required for virtualization networking
  capability net_admin,
  capability net_raw,
  capability setuid,
  capability setgid,

  # Proc and sys access needed by QEMU
  @{PROC}/sys/net/** r,
  @{PROC}/*/stat r,
  @{PROC}/*/status r,
  /sys/devices/** r,
  /sys/class/** r,

  # Temporary and log files
  /tmp/** rw,
  /var/tmp/** rw,
  /var/log/** w,
}
EOF'
Load the profile and confirm it is active:

oc debug node/worker-0 -- chroot /host apparmor_parser -r /etc/apparmor.d/kubevirt-vm
oc debug node/worker-0 -- chroot /host bash -c 'aa-status | grep kubevirt-vm'
Expected output:

   kubevirt-vm
This confirms the profile name appears in the loaded-profiles list under aa-status.

Step 2.3: Create a VM Annotated to Use the AppArmor Profile
cat > apparmor-secured-vm.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: kubevirt.io/v1
kind: VirtualMachine
metadata:
  name: apparmor-secured-vm
  namespace: default
spec:
  running: false
  template:
    metadata:
      annotations:
        container.apparmor.security.beta.kubernetes.io/compute: localhost/kubevirt-vm
      labels:
        kubevirt.io/vm: apparmor-secured-vm
        security-profile: apparmor-enabled
    spec:
      domain:
        cpu:
          cores: 1
        devices:
          disks:
          - disk:
              bus: virtio
            name: containerdisk
          - disk:
              bus: virtio
            name: cloudinitdisk
          interfaces:
          - masquerade: {}
            name: default
        resources:
          requests:
            memory: 1Gi
      networks:
      - name: default
        pod: {}
      volumes:
      - containerDisk:
          image: quay.io/kubevirt/fedora-cloud-container-disk-demo
        name: containerdisk
      - cloudInitNoCloud:
          userData: |
            #cloud-config
            user: fedora
            password: fedora
            chpasswd: { expire: False }
        name: cloudinitdisk
EOF

oc apply -f apparmor-secured-vm.yaml
oc patch vm apparmor-secured-vm --type merge -p '{"spec":{"running":true}}'
oc get vmi apparmor-secured-vm -w
Wait for PHASE to show Running, then press Ctrl+C.

Step 2.4: Verify the AppArmor Profile Is Applied to the Pod
APPARMOR_VM_POD=$(oc get pods -l kubevirt.io/vm=apparmor-secured-vm -o jsonpath='{.items[0].metadata.name}')
oc get pod $APPARMOR_VM_POD -o jsonpath='{.metadata.annotations.container\.apparmor\.security\.beta\.kubernetes\.io/compute}{"\n"}'
Expected output:

localhost/kubevirt-vm
This confirms the pod was admitted with the AppArmor annotation and the node applied the kubevirt-vm profile to the compute container (the container that runs the QEMU process).

Deliverable for Task 2: the aa-status output showing kubevirt-vm loaded, and the annotation query output showing localhost/kubevirt-vm applied to the running pod.

Task 3: Audit Security Enforcement Events
Step 3.1: Search for SELinux Denials Related to Virtualization
oc debug node/worker-0 -- chroot /host ausearch -m avc -ts recent 2>&1 | grep -i qemu
Expected outcome: if the audit log has no matches, ausearch prints <no matches>, which is the correct and healthy result for a properly functioning VM. If denials are present, each entry includes fields such as type=AVC, denied { read }, scontext= and tcontext=, showing which SELinux type was denied access to which resource.

Step 3.2: Write a Simple Audit Summary Script
cat > /tmp/vm-security-audit.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash
set -uo pipefail

echo "=== VM Security Audit Report ==="
echo "Generated on: $(date)"
echo

echo "1. SELinux Status:"
oc debug node/worker-0 -- chroot /host sestatus 2>/dev/null | grep -E "SELinux status|Current mode"
echo

echo "2. AppArmor Profiles Loaded:"
oc debug node/worker-0 -- chroot /host bash -c 'aa-status | head -5' 2>/dev/null
echo

echo "3. VMs and Their Security Labels:"
oc get vm -n default -o custom-columns="NAME:.metadata.name,SECURITY_PROFILE:.spec.template.metadata.labels.security-profile" --no-headers
echo

echo "4. Recent SELinux Denials Matching qemu:"
oc debug node/worker-0 -- chroot /host ausearch -m avc -ts recent 2>&1 | grep -i qemu || echo "No denials found"
echo

echo "=== End of Security Audit ==="
EOF

chmod +x /tmp/vm-security-audit.sh
/tmp/vm-security-audit.sh | tee /tmp/security-audit-report.txt
Expected outcome: the script prints all four sections without shell errors, and the report is saved to /tmp/security-audit-report.txt for review.

Deliverable for Task 3: the saved /tmp/security-audit-report.txt file containing the four numbered sections, with Section 3 showing secure-vm (no security profile) alongside apparmor-secured-vm (apparmor-enabled), demonstrating the contrast between an unhardened and a hardened workload.

Expected Outcomes
A running KubeVirt VM whose QEMU process context (user:role:type:level) has been inspected and correctly interpreted, including the role of MCS categories in pod isolation
A custom AppArmor profile loaded on a worker node and successfully attached to a VM's virt-launcher pod via annotation, verified by inspecting the pod's applied annotations
A working audit script that summarizes SELinux mode, loaded AppArmor profiles, VM security labels, and recent denials in one report
Troubleshooting
Issue 1: ps -eZ shows no SELinux context or shows unconfined_t for the QEMU process. Symptom: the type field is unconfined_t instead of a confined type like container_t. This suggests the node's SELinux mode may not be enforcing, or the pod was created before SELinux labeling was applied correctly. Diagnostic hint: re-run sestatus on the same node and confirm Current mode: enforcing, and check oc get pod <pod> -o yaml for the securityContext section to see what SELinux options the pod requested.

Issue 2: The AppArmor annotation is present on the VirtualMachine but missing from the running pod, or the pod fails to schedule. Symptom: oc describe pod <pod> shows an event referencing AppArmor profile not found. Diagnostic hint: confirm the profile was loaded on the same node the pod was scheduled to, since apparmor_parser -r only applies to the node it was run on; check oc get pod <pod> -o jsonpath='{.spec.nodeName}' and compare it to the node where you loaded the profile.

Conclusion
In this lab, students verified SELinux enforcement on OpenShift Virtualization nodes, inspected and correctly interpreted the SELinux context of a live QEMU process, and built and loaded a custom AppArmor profile that was successfully attached to a KubeVirt virtual machine through pod annotations. Students also built a repeatable audit script that summarizes SELinux mode, loaded AppArmor profiles, and VM security labeling in a single report, giving them a practical baseline for ongoing security monitoring. Together these exercises reinforce a core defensive security principle for virtualization platforms: mandatory access controls such as SELinux and AppArmor provide layered, kernel-enforced isolation between VM workloads that complements Kubernetes-level access controls, and both layers must be verified and audited rather than assumed to be working correctly by default.
